            <ArticleTitle>Adjusting background noise in cluster analyses of longitudinal data.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>93-104</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.csda.2016.11.009</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Background noise in cluster analyses can potentially mask the true underlying patterns. To tease out patterns uniquely to certain populations, a Bayesian semi-parametric clustering method is presented. It infers and adjusts background noise. The method is built upon a mixture of the Dirichlet process and a point mass function. Simulations demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed method. The method is then applied to analyze a longitudinal data set on allergic sensitization and asthma status.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
